1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,995
Pay a friend fifty rupees on your phone.

2
00:00:02,995 --> 00:00:04,385
Two messages pop up.

3
00:00:04,385 --> 00:00:06,147
Yours says, fifty debited.

4
00:00:06,147 --> 00:00:08,338
Your friend's says, fifty credited.

5
00:00:08,738 --> 00:00:10,280
Two halves of one deal.

6
00:00:10,280 --> 00:00:13,492
Each half makes sense alone,
and together they match.

7
00:00:13,942 --> 00:00:15,552
Aarav is studying late.

8
00:00:15,552 --> 00:00:19,884
His long equation will not balance,
however he shuffles the numbers.

9
00:00:20,234 --> 00:00:24,021
So he cuts it in two, and
balances one half at a time.

10
00:00:24,521 --> 00:00:26,550
That is a real chemistry trick.

11
00:00:26,550 --> 00:00:30,421
By the end, you will balance a
messy equation in clean steps.

12
00:00:31,071 --> 00:00:32,484
Here is the idea.

13
00:00:32,484 --> 00:00:35,025
Every redox reaction has two halves.

14
00:00:35,375 --> 00:00:37,442
The oxidation half gives out electrons.

15
00:00:37,442 --> 00:00:39,267
The reduction half takes them in.

16
00:00:39,667 --> 00:00:42,921
We write each half on its
own, with the electrons shown.

17
00:00:42,921 --> 00:00:44,108
Then we join them.

18
00:00:44,708 --> 00:00:47,646
Let's balance one on our molecule bench.

19
00:00:47,646 --> 00:00:50,752
Iron two plus meets dichromate, in acid.

20
00:00:51,102 --> 00:00:52,002
Split it.

21
00:00:51,911 --> 00:00:55,452
Oxidation half: iron two
plus becomes iron three plus.

22
00:00:55,852 --> 00:00:59,084
Reduction half: dichromate
becomes chromium three plus.

23
00:00:59,484 --> 00:01:00,733
Iron half first.

24
00:01:00,733 --> 00:01:02,407
One iron on each side.

25
00:01:02,407 --> 00:01:07,004
The charge goes from plus two to
plus three, so one electron leaves.

26
00:01:07,404 --> 00:01:09,392
Each iron gives one electron.

27
00:01:09,392 --> 00:01:11,082
Hold on to that number.

28
00:01:11,682 --> 00:01:13,505
Now the dichromate half.

29
00:01:13,505 --> 00:01:14,670
Step by step.

30
00:01:15,020 --> 00:01:15,920
Step one.

31
00:01:15,809 --> 00:01:16,982
Balance chromium.

32
00:01:16,982 --> 00:01:19,608
Put two in front of chromium three plus.

33
00:01:20,358 --> 00:01:21,258
Step two.

34
00:01:21,192 --> 00:01:23,013
Balance oxygen with water.

35
00:01:23,013 --> 00:01:25,993
Seven oxygen, so seven water on the right.

36
00:01:26,743 --> 00:01:27,683
Step three.

37
00:01:27,683 --> 00:01:29,725
Balance hydrogen with H plus.

38
00:01:29,725 --> 00:01:33,186
Fourteen hydrogen, so
fourteen H plus on the left.

39
00:01:33,936 --> 00:01:34,836
Step four.

40
00:01:34,660 --> 00:01:36,532
Balance the charge with electrons.

41
00:01:36,532 --> 00:01:38,245
The left side is plus twelve.

42
00:01:38,245 --> 00:01:39,875
The right side is plus six.

43
00:01:39,875 --> 00:01:41,631
Add six electrons on the left.

44
00:01:42,381 --> 00:01:43,743
Now join the halves.

45
00:01:43,743 --> 00:01:45,610
Dichromate takes six electrons.

46
00:01:45,610 --> 00:01:46,973
Each iron gives one.

47
00:01:47,373 --> 00:01:49,923
So multiply the iron half by six.

48
00:01:49,923 --> 00:01:51,727
Six given, six taken.

49
00:01:51,727 --> 00:01:53,310
The scale is level.

50
00:01:53,710 --> 00:01:54,658
Add the halves.

51
00:01:54,658 --> 00:01:56,057
The electrons cancel out.

52
00:01:56,057 --> 00:01:58,123
They never appear in the final answer.

53
00:01:58,623 --> 00:02:03,545
One dichromate, six iron two plus
and fourteen H plus give two chromium

54
00:02:03,545 --> 00:02:07,160
three plus, six iron three
plus and seven water.

55
00:02:07,810 --> 00:02:10,000
This is the half reaction method.

56
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:12,742
It is also called the ion electron method.

57
00:02:13,092 --> 00:02:15,093
Electrons are written openly in each half.

58
00:02:15,093 --> 00:02:17,687
Then the electrons given must
equal the electrons taken.

59
00:02:18,337 --> 00:02:21,195
Now compare acid and base side by side.

60
00:02:21,195 --> 00:02:25,695
In acid, we balance oxygen with
water and hydrogen with H plus.

61
00:02:26,095 --> 00:02:28,827
In a base, first balance as if in acid.

62
00:02:28,827 --> 00:02:32,559
Then add one OH minus for
every H plus, on both sides.

63
00:02:32,559 --> 00:02:34,185
Each pair becomes water.

64
00:02:34,685 --> 00:02:35,692
Pause and think.

65
00:02:35,692 --> 00:02:39,320
After adding the two halves, should
any electrons be left in the final

66
00:02:39,320 --> 00:02:40,220
equation?

67
00:02:43,142 --> 00:02:44,042
No.

68
00:02:43,524 --> 00:02:46,962
Electrons given equal electrons
taken, so they cancel completely.

69
00:02:47,612 --> 00:02:48,512
Remember this.

70
00:02:48,475 --> 00:02:51,609
Split, balance each half,
match the electrons, then add.

71
00:02:52,309 --> 00:02:55,110
Now, one worked example,
in a basic solution.

72
00:02:55,410 --> 00:02:59,943
Permanganate turns iodide into
iodine, and becomes manganese dioxide.

73
00:03:00,443 --> 00:03:01,448
Oxidation half.

74
00:03:01,448 --> 00:03:04,121
Two iodide give one
iodine and two electrons.

75
00:03:04,871 --> 00:03:07,482
Reduction half, balanced as if in acid.

76
00:03:07,482 --> 00:03:11,715
Permanganate, four H plus and three
electrons give manganese dioxide and

77
00:03:11,715 --> 00:03:12,615
two water.

78
00:03:13,300 --> 00:03:17,085
It is basic, so add four
OH minus to both sides.

79
00:03:17,085 --> 00:03:20,591
Four H plus and four OH
minus make four water.

80
00:03:21,341 --> 00:03:22,668
Cancel two water.

81
00:03:22,668 --> 00:03:27,076
Permanganate, two water and three
electrons give manganese dioxide and

82
00:03:27,076 --> 00:03:28,362
four OH minus.

83
00:03:29,112 --> 00:03:30,448
Match the electrons.

84
00:03:30,448 --> 00:03:33,699
Iodide half times three,
permanganate half times two.

85
00:03:33,699 --> 00:03:34,661
Six and six.

86
00:03:35,411 --> 00:03:40,392
Two permanganate, six iodide and four
water give two manganese dioxide,

87
00:03:40,392 --> 00:03:42,849
three iodine and eight hydroxide.

88
00:03:43,499 --> 00:03:44,963
Now, the exam trap.

89
00:03:45,313 --> 00:03:48,974
Students add electrons first, before
balancing oxygen and hydrogen.

90
00:03:48,974 --> 00:03:50,882
Then the charge comes out wrong.

91
00:03:51,282 --> 00:03:52,214
Atoms first.

92
00:03:52,214 --> 00:03:55,712
The main atom, then oxygen
with water, then hydrogen.

93
00:03:55,712 --> 00:03:57,086
Electrons come last.

94
00:03:57,736 --> 00:03:59,177
Where is this asked?

95
00:03:59,177 --> 00:04:02,561
Boards ask you to balance
by the ion electron method.

96
00:04:02,561 --> 00:04:06,932
Entrance tests ask for the electrons,
water or hydroxide in one half.

97
00:04:07,532 --> 00:04:08,663
Exam level now.

98
00:04:08,663 --> 00:04:12,640
In a basic solution, chromium
hydroxide is oxidised to chromate.

99
00:04:12,640 --> 00:04:16,972
In the balanced oxidation half, how
many electrons and how many hydroxide

100
00:04:16,972 --> 00:04:17,901
ions appear?

101
00:04:17,901 --> 00:04:19,080
Pause and solve.

102
00:04:24,480 --> 00:04:25,674
The answer is A.

103
00:04:25,674 --> 00:04:27,804
Three electrons and five hydroxide.

104
00:04:28,304 --> 00:04:30,778
Chromium goes from plus three to plus six.

105
00:04:30,778 --> 00:04:32,710
So three electrons on the right.

106
00:04:33,210 --> 00:04:34,110
Charge.

107
00:04:33,845 --> 00:04:37,717
The right side is minus two
and minus three, so minus five.

108
00:04:37,717 --> 00:04:39,798
Add five hydroxide on the left.

109
00:04:40,298 --> 00:04:43,443
Four water on the right
balances hydrogen and oxygen.

110
00:04:43,443 --> 00:04:45,748
B forgot the charge of the electrons.

111
00:04:46,348 --> 00:04:47,889
Here is your recap.

112
00:04:47,889 --> 00:04:49,473
Screenshot this card.

113
00:04:52,673 --> 00:04:54,859
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

114
00:04:55,259 --> 00:04:56,159
One.

115
00:04:55,687 --> 00:04:58,540
What are the two halves of
a redox reaction called?

116
00:04:59,340 --> 00:05:00,240
Two.

117
00:04:59,674 --> 00:05:01,399
In which step do we add the electrons?

118
00:05:02,199 --> 00:05:03,099
Three.

119
00:05:02,691 --> 00:05:05,518
How do you turn an acid
balance into a base balance?

120
00:05:06,418 --> 00:05:10,738
Next time: how can one pink drop tell
a chemist that a reaction is over?

121
00:05:10,738 --> 00:05:12,706
We find out in redox titrations.
